1.1. Overview

   This module provides a DB APIv1 connector that carries database
   operations over NATS request/reply instead of talking to a database
   server directly. A backend service subscribed to the configured
   subjects executes the operations and replies.

   Because it implements the generic Kamailio database interface, any
   module that takes a db_url can use it - usrloc, auth_db, permissions,
   htable, dialog and so on.

   A reference backend implementation in Go, which executes the requests
   against MySQL, is shipped in misc/external/db_nats/.

   Operations are sent as structured, typed JSON and the backend binds the
   values as query parameters. Values are therefore never concatenated
   into SQL. This differs from a driver such as db_mysql, which quotes
   values locally with mysql_real_escape_string(); db_nats has no local
   database connection and so cannot escape safely on its own.

1.2. Limitations

     * No transactions. START TRANSACTION, COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT,
       SET AUTOCOMMIT, SET SESSION, LOCK TABLES, GET_LOCK() and SELECT ...
       FOR UPDATE all require a connection that persists across calls.
       Over stateless request/reply each statement may reach a different
       backend and a different database connection. See the
       session_sql_mode parameter.
     * No LAST_INSERT_ID, INSERT DELAYED or async queries. These entry
       points are not implemented. Every module in the tree null-tests
       them, so nothing breaks by their absence.
     * Every operation blocks the calling process until the reply arrives
       or request_timeout expires. With usrloc db_mode=3 this puts a round
       trip on every lookup and save.
     * Some modules load an entire table in a single query with no fetch
       fallback (for example permissions). The NATS max_payload must be
       large enough for those replies. Modules that honour DB_CAP_FETCH,
       such as usrloc, page through a server-side cursor instead and are
       not affected.

1.3. Dependencies

   The NATS C client library (nats.c, version 2.0 or later) must be
   installed. Unlike the nats module, db_nats does not need libuv: it uses
   synchronous request/reply rather than an event loop.

1.4. Database URL

   The URL gives the NATS server and the subject namespace:
nats://[username:password@]host[:port]/[subject_prefix]

   The path selects the subject prefix; when omitted, the subject_prefix
   parameter is used. Three subjects are derived from it:
     * <prefix>.op - structured operations
     * <prefix>.raw - raw SQL passthrough
     * <prefix>.cursor - fetch continuation and cursor close

   They are separate so that NATS account permissions can deny the raw
   subject for a given credential, keeping enforcement on the bus.

   Example 1. Using db_nats
modparam("usrloc",  "db_url", "nats://127.0.0.1:4222/kamailio.db")
modparam("auth_db", "db_url", "nats://127.0.0.1:4222/kamailio.db")

1.5. Parameters

1.5.1. nats_url (string)

   A NATS server to connect to. May be set several times to give a
   cluster, up to 10 entries, in the same style as the nats module.

   The srdb1 database URL cannot express a server list: a comma in db_url
   is not parsed as a separator but scattered across the host, password
   and database fields, which would silently connect to the wrong place.
   db_nats therefore refuses any db_url containing a comma and expects the
   cluster here instead.

   When at least one nats_url is set, the host in db_url is ignored
   entirely and only its path is used, to select the subject prefix. Give
   it a placeholder host in that case.

   Credentials may be embedded per server. They are masked in log output.

   Example 2. A cluster with credentials
modparam("db_nats", "nats_url", "nats://user:pass@10.0.0.1:4222")
modparam("db_nats", "nats_url", "nats://user:pass@10.0.0.2:4222")
modparam("db_nats", "nats_url", "nats://user:pass@10.0.0.3:4222")

# host is a placeholder here; only the path is used
modparam("usrloc", "db_url", "nats://cluster/kamailio.db")

1.5.2. request_timeout (int)

   How long to wait for a reply, in milliseconds. The calling process is
   blocked for this period, so on a SIP worker it is a stall budget: keep
   it well below your transaction timers.

   Default value is 1000 (1 second).

   Example 3. Set request_timeout
modparam("db_nats", "request_timeout", 500)

1.5.3. connect_timeout (int)

   Connection establishment timeout, in milliseconds.

   Default value is 2000.

1.5.4. error_penalty (int)

   After a transport error, fail subsequent requests immediately for this
   many milliseconds instead of attempting them. Set to 0 to disable.

   Without it, every query during an outage waits out request_timeout, and
   each one blocks the calling process for that long. With it, one request
   per window probes the connection and the rest fail at once.

   Note this state is per process: the connection lives in private memory
   and is keyed by pid, so each worker keeps its own window. The cost of
   an outage is therefore roughly one timeout per window per process, not
   one globally.

   Default value is 1000.

   Example 4. Set error_penalty
modparam("db_nats", "error_penalty", 2000)

1.5.5. error_threshold (int)

   How many consecutive transport errors a process must see before it
   opens an error_penalty window. Raise it to tolerate isolated blips at
   the cost of more requests waiting out the full timeout.

   Default value is 1.

1.5.6. session_sql_mode (string)

   What to do when a raw query carries session state (COMMIT, SET
   AUTOCOMMIT, LOCK TABLES and similar). Either reject or ignore.
     * reject - the call fails with an error.
     * ignore - the call succeeds as a no-op and a warning is logged. Use
       this only when the caller can tolerate losing atomicity and
       isolation.

   In both cases the statement is never sent. There is deliberately no
   forwarding mode: a forwarded SET AUTOCOMMIT=0 would leave a pooled
   backend connection with autocommit disabled for every later unrelated
   query, while the matching COMMIT reached a different connection.

   Default value is "reject".

   Example 5. Set session_sql_mode
modparam("db_nats", "session_sql_mode", "ignore")

1.5.7. subject_prefix (string)

   Subject prefix used when the database URL has no path.

   Default value is "kamailio.db".

1.5.8. max_reconnect (int)

   Maximum reconnect attempts made by the NATS client.

   Default value is 10000.

1.5.9. reconnect_wait (int)

   Delay between reconnect attempts, in milliseconds.

   Default value is 2000.

1.6. Result size and private memory

   Replies are parsed into private (pkg) memory, and a JSON document costs
   considerably more than its wire size once parsed into nodes and copied
   into result values. Budget roughly 40 to 50 times the reply size in pkg
   memory.

   This matters for modules that page through large tables. With usrloc's
   default fetch_rows of 2000, a page of the location table is around 450
   kB on the wire and needs about 24 MB of pkg to decode - more than the 8
   MB default. The symptom is a failed to parse reply json error during
   preload.

   Either lower fetch_rows (500 works comfortably within the default pkg
   for the location table) or raise pkg memory with the -M command line
   option.

   Separately, a reply must fit the NATS server's max_payload, 1 MB by
   default. Modules that page through a cursor are naturally bounded by
   their fetch size. Modules that load a whole table in one query and have
   no fetch fallback, such as permissions, are not: for those the backend
   answers with a reply_too_large error rather than leaving the request to
   time out, and max_payload must be raised or the query narrowed.

1.7. Running more than one backend

   Backends subscribe as a queue group, so ordinary operations are spread
   across every instance. Cursors are the exception: an open cursor holds
   a live result set inside one backend process and cannot be served by
   another. The reply that opens a cursor therefore carries a
   cursor_subject naming its owner, and db_nats addresses all continuation
   there rather than to the shared subject.

   A backend that does not send cursor_subject still works, but only when
   a single instance is running: with more than one, fetches are load
   balanced away from the cursor's owner and fail with a message saying
   the cursor is no longer available.

   If the owning instance stops, an open cursor is lost. The fetch fails
   promptly rather than hanging, and the operation in progress - a usrloc
   preload, for example - is reported as failed. db_nats deliberately does
   not reopen the query behind the caller's back, since restarting a
   partly consumed result would silently skip or duplicate rows.

   A backend being restarted deliberately does not have this effect: on
   SIGTERM it stops accepting new work but keeps serving already-open
   cursors until they finish or its grace period expires, so a preload in
   progress completes rather than failing.

   One queue group must serve exactly one logical database. Operations are
   load balanced, so backends in the same group pointing at different
   databases would answer the same query from different data,
   non-deterministically. Give each database its own subject prefix,
   selected per module through the path of db_url. Read replicas of one
   database are equally unsuitable, because a cursor is bound to a single
   connection's snapshot.

1.8. Module compatibility

   Two modules drive SQL transactions through raw queries and cannot work
   correctly without connection affinity: ims_usrloc_scscf and p_usrloc.
   Note that p_usrloc's failover path is not gated by its
   db_use_transactions parameter, so disabling that is not sufficient.

   At startup db_nats inspects those modules' configured database URLs. If
   one of them actually points at db_nats, startup fails with an
   explanatory error when session_sql_mode is reject, or logs a warning
   when it is ignore. A module pointed at a different driver is left
   alone.

   sqlops requires DB_CAP_RAW_QUERY at startup, so the raw subject must be
   reachable and the backend must permit raw statements for it to work.

1.9. Backend contract

   Two encoding rules matter when writing your own backend:
     * Every value travels as a JSON string, never a JSON number. The
       module parses replies with srjson, which stores all numbers as
       double, so a 64-bit integer sent as a number would be silently
       corrupted above 2^53.
     * Values of type datetime are unix epoch seconds in both directions.

   Column types must be reported accurately. In particular text columns
   must be declared string and not str, because only the former is
   NUL-terminated when copied, and modules such as usrloc and auth_db read
   values with VAL_STRING() and strlen(). A SQL NULL is sent as a JSON
   null in the row array.

   The backend must also serve the version table with an integer version
   column, since most modules call db_check_table_version() at startup.
